WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.523
TritonAI Insights lets you ask a question

2
00:00:03.563 --> 00:00:06.530
about UC San Diego in plain English

3
00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:08.890
and get a sourced answer in seconds.

4
00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:11.720
In the next six minutes, you'll see how to
ask,

5
00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:13.120
how to read an answer,

6
00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:14.362
and how to tell when a figure

7
00:00:14.402 --> 00:00:16.260
is ready to use in a briefing.

8
00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:19.700
Insights covers six areas today:

9
00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:22.970
Research, Finance, Workforce, Students,
Rankings,

10
00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:26.720
and questions that cut across them.

11
00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:29.880
Behind them are reports the campus already

12
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.450
relies on in Tableau and Cognos:

13
00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:35.266
research and finance from the Activity
Hubs,

14
00:00:35.306 --> 00:00:39.110
budget against actuals from the Campus
Budget Office, employee rosters,

15
00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:43.730
student outcomes from Institutional
Research and the UC Information Center,

16
00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.520
the national HERD research survey, and the
published rankings.

17
00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.660
For research, finance and workforce,

18
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:53.590
Insights also queries the campus data
warehouse live,

19
00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:55.640
wherever its connections cover the
question.

20
00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:59.000
Everything else comes from dated copies of
those reports.

21
00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:00.690
Facilities is next.

22
00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:04.170
The AI that writes the answers runs on
TritonAI,

23
00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:06.800
UC San Diego's own campus service,

24
00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:09.530
so no outside AI company sees this data.

25
00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:11.320
And it only reads;

26
00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:13.930
it never changes anything in campus
systems.

27
00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.210
The easiest way to start is

28
00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.050
one of these suggested questions.

29
00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:22.710
Each one marked Ready was answered ahead
of time

30
00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:26.650
and checked figure by figure against the
source reports,

31
00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:28.860
so it appears instantly.

32
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:32.960
Here, campus indirect cost recovery fell
11.8 percent last year,

33
00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:37.680
to 343 million dollars, after three years
of growth.

34
00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:41.160
Every answer reads the same way:

35
00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:42.740
the headline figure,

36
00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:46.330
then a few sentences that end with the
definitions used.

37
00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:49.790
Here, IDC means what was actually
recovered,

38
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.020
not what was budgeted.

39
00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:54.090
Then a chart, and the table behind it.

40
00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:55.960
Below that are the sources:

41
00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:57.090
which report, which system,

42
00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:59.750
and the date the data was taken.

43
00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:03.080
"How this was calculated" lists the
assumptions,

44
00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.100
and "Show the SQL" shows the exact query,

45
00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:07.694
so an analyst in your area

46
00:02:07.734 --> 00:02:10.130
can check it or repeat it.

47
00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:12.810
The footer says this is a prepared answer,

48
00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:14.640
and gives the date of the data

49
00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:16.290
and the date the figures were checked.

50
00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:17.970
And "Copy table" puts the figures on

51
00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:20.790
your clipboard for a briefing note.

52
00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:26.120
You can also type any question in your own
words.

53
00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:29.520
Questions that weren't prepared ahead are
answered live,

54
00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:34.260
usually in 10 to 30 seconds, and they're
labelled Exploratory.

55
00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.430
The figures come from real queries on
campus data,

56
00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:39.790
often straight from the data warehouse,

57
00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:42.080
but they haven't been checked one by one.

58
00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:45.330
Treat an Exploratory answer as a strong
first draft.

59
00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.202
Before you share a figure or

60
00:02:47.242 --> 00:02:49.740
use it in a budget decision,

61
00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:52.170
confirm it against the source listed
underneath,

62
00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:54.250
or with the office that owns the data.

63
00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:56.380
If a question could mean two things,

64
00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:59.240
Insights asks which you meant rather than
guessing.

65
00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:01.220
If the data isn't there, it says so,

66
00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:03.810
and names the office that holds it.

67
00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:10.760
Each answer suggests three next questions,
marked drill down, compare,

68
00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:12.450
trend or risk.

69
00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:15.840
You can also type a follow-up, like "and
by school?",

70
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.110
and Insights uses the conversation so far
as context.

71
00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:22.940
New topic clears that context, and New
chat starts over.

72
00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:27.720
Some of the most useful questions cross
areas.

73
00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:31.010
This one puts each school's share of
research overhead,

74
00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:33.830
course enrollments and faculty side by
side.

75
00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:37.940
The School of Medicine brings in 56
percent of research

76
00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.250
IDC with two and a half percent of course
enrollments,

77
00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:44.000
while Social Sciences teaches about a
quarter of all enrollments.

78
00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:45.750
Rankings works the same way.

79
00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:49.017
UC San Diego placed 29th overall in U.S.

80
00:03:49.057 --> 00:03:52.550
News this year, tied for sixth among
public universities.

81
00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:55.460
Insights shows which inputs carry the most
weight,

82
00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:58.020
and where the gaps to peers are largest,

83
00:03:58.070 --> 00:03:59.780
such as the six-year graduation rate.

84
00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:03.350
A few ground rules.

85
00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:06.620
Ready answers are fine to use in a
briefing;

86
00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:10.480
Exploratory answers need a check before
they go further.

87
00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:12.367
Keep the definition and the date

88
00:04:12.407 --> 00:04:14.110
with any figure you pass on.

89
00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:15.590
The footer gives you both.

90
00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:18.880
Live answers come straight from the campus
data warehouse;

91
00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:22.330
other figures come from dated copies of
campus reports.

92
00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:25.690
Every answer names its source and date,

93
00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.780
and that source report is the reference.

94
00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.230
It's for understanding the campus, not for
evaluating individual people.

95
00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:34.125
Results that name faculty or show

96
00:04:34.165 --> 00:04:36.910
pay are confidential to this group,

97
00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:39.310
so please don't forward screenshots of
them.

98
00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:40.880
And access is personal.

99
00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:42.690
It's granted person by person,

100
00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:44.870
so if someone on your team needs it,

101
00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:47.890
ask the Insights team rather than sharing
your sign-in.

102
00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:51.070
To get started,

103
00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:58.630
open insights.tritonai.ucsd.edu and sign
in with your campus single sign-on.

104
00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:01.390
It works on a laptop or a phone browser.

105
00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:04.720
Try a Ready question in an area you know
well,

106
00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:07.240
and check that the answer matches what
you'd expect.

107
00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:10.210
Then tell the Insights team what you find:

108
00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:14.130
a figure that looks wrong, a question
that's missing,

109
00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:16.070
or one you'd like prepared.

110
00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:18.970
That feedback is how Insights gets better.

111
00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:21.130
Thank you.
